T222	Cue 139616 139625	exclaimed
T223	Entity 139626 139635	the widow
T224	Quotation 139637 139918	"Since that one is coming to pieces, Madame Lefrancois. I tell you again you are doing yourself harm, much harm! And besides, players now want narrow pockets and heavy cues. Hazards aren't played now; everything is changed! One must keep pace with the times! Just look at Tellier!"
R207	Cueing Arg1:T222 Arg2:T221	
R208	Speaker Arg1:T223 Arg2:T221	
R209	Addressee Arg1:T219 Arg2:T224	
R210	Addressee Arg1:T219 Arg2:T221	
R211	Cueing Arg1:T222 Arg2:T224	
R212	Speaker Arg1:T223 Arg2:T224	
T225	Quotation 139977 140161	"You may say what you like; his table is better than yours; and if one were to think, for example, of getting up a patriotic pool for Poland or the sufferers from the Lyons floods -- "
T226	Quotation 140162 140210	"It isn't beggars like him that'll frighten us,"
T227	Cue 140211 140222	interrupted
T228	Entity 140223 140235	the landlady
T229	Entity 139919 139930	The hostess
T230	Entity 139955 139966	The chemist
R213	Addressee Arg1:T229 Arg2:T225	
T231	Cue 139967 139974	went on
R214	Cueing Arg1:T231 Arg2:T225	
R215	Speaker Arg1:T230 Arg2:T225	
R216	Cueing Arg1:T227 Arg2:T226	
R217	Speaker Arg1:T228 Arg2:T226	
R218	Addressee Arg1:T230 Arg2:T226	
T232	Quotation 140266 140703	"Come, come, Monsieur Homais; as long as the 'Lion d'Or' exists people will come to it. We've feathered our nest; while one of these days you'll find the 'Cafe Francais' closed with a big placard on the shutters. Change my billiard-table!" she went on, speaking to herself, "the table that comes in so handy for folding the washing, and on which, in the hunting season, I have slept six visitors! But that dawdler, Hivert, doesn't come!"
R219	Speaker Arg1:T228 Arg2:T232	
R220	Cueing Arg1:T227 Arg2:T232	
R221	Addressee Arg1:T230 Arg2:T232	
T233	Quotation 140704 140758	"Are you waiting for him for your gentlemen's dinner?"
T234	Quotation 140759 141233	"Wait for him! And what about Monsieur Binet? As the clock strikes six you'll see him come in, for he hasn't his equal under the sun for punctuality. He must always have his seat in the small parlour. He'd rather die than dine anywhere else. And so squeamish as he is, and so particular about the cider! Not like Monsieur Leon; he sometimes comes at seven, or even half-past, and he doesn't so much as look at what he eats. Such a nice young man! Never speaks a rough word!"
T235	Quotation 141234 141351	"Well, you see, there's a great difference between an educated man and an old carabineer who is now a tax-collector."
R222	Speaker Arg1:T230 Arg2:T235	
R223	Addressee Arg1:T230 Arg2:T234	
R224	Speaker Arg1:T230 Arg2:T233	
R225	Addressee Arg1:T228 Arg2:T235	
R226	Speaker Arg1:T228 Arg2:T234	
R227	Addressee Arg1:T228 Arg2:T233	
T236	Quotation 142503 142570	"It isn't with saying civil things that he'll wear out his tongue,"
T237	Cue 142571 142575	said
T238	Entity 142576 142587	the chemist
T239	Quotation 142632 142654	"He never talks more,"
T240	Entity 142655 142658	she
T241	Cue 142659 142666	replied
T242	Quotation 142668 142870	"Last week two travelers in the cloth line were here-- such clever chaps who told such jokes in the evening, that I fairly cried with laughing; and he stood there like a dab fish and never said a word."
T243	Quotation 142871 142877	"Yes,"
T244	Cue 142878 142886	observed
T245	Entity 142887 142898	the chemist
T246	Quotation 142900 142965	"no imagination, no sallies, nothing that makes the society-man."
T247	Quotation 142966 142994	"Yet they say he has parts,"
T248	Cue 142995 143003	objected
T249	Quotation 143018 143026	"Parts!"
T250	Cue 143027 143034	replied
T251	Entity 143035 143050	Monsieur Homais
T252	Quotation 143052 143096	"he, parts! In his own line it is possible,"
T253	Entity 143097 143099	he
T254	Cue 143100 143105	added
T255	Entity 143128 143130	he
T256	Quotation 143141 143579	"Ah! That a merchant, who has large connections, a jurisconsult, a doctor, a chemist, should be thus absent-minded, that they should become whimsical or even peevish, I can understand; such cases are cited in history. But at least it is because they are thinking of something. Myself, for example, how often has it happened to me to look on the bureau for my pen to write a label, and to find, after all, that I had put it behind my ear!"
T257	Quotation 143835 143878	"What can I do for you, Monsieur le Curie?"
T258	Cue 143879 143884	asked
T259	Entity 143885 143897	the landlady
T260	Quotation 144003 144070	"Will you take something? A thimbleful of Cassis? A glass of wine?"
T261	Quotation 144716 144892	"Besides, he could double up four men like you over his knee. Last year he helped our people to bring in the straw; he carried as many as six trusses at once, he is so strong."
T262	Quotation 144893 144901	"Bravo!"
T263	Cue 144902 144906	said
T264	Entity 144907 144918	the chemist
T265	Quotation 144920 145168	"Now just send your daughters to confess to fellows which such a temperament! I, if I were the Government, I'd have the priests bled once a month. Yes, Madame Lefrancois, every month -- a good phlebotomy, in the interests of the police and morals."
T266	Quotation 145169 145237	"Be quiet, Monsieur Homais. You are an infidel; you've no religion."
T267	Quotation 145260 146431	"I have a religion, my religion, and I even have more than all these others with their mummeries and their juggling. I adore God, on the contrary. I believe in the Supreme Being, in a Creator, whatever he may be. I care little who has placed us here below to fulfil our duties as citizens and fathers of families; but I don't need to go to church to kiss silver plates, and fatten, out of my pocket, a lot of good-for-nothings who live better than we do. For one can know Him as well in a wood, in a field, or even contemplating the eternal vault like the ancients. My God! Mine is the God of Socrates, of Franklin, of Voltaire, and of Beranger! I am for the profession of faith of the 'Savoyard Vicar,' and the immortal principles of '89! And I can't admit of an old boy of a God who takes walks in his garden with a cane in his hand, who lodges his friends in the belly of whales, dies uttering a cry, and rises again at the end of three days; things absurd in themselves, and completely opposed, moreover, to all physical laws, which prove to us, by the way, that priests have always wallowed in turpid ignorance, in which they would fain engulf the people with them."
T268	Entity 145238 145249	The chemist
T269	Cue 145250 145258	answered
